# ffi/logs/rust/stage3-content-sets.log
# W3 stage 3: the latin1 and wide content sets on the string path, M1 (P1.2) and
# M2 (P2.2). ABI v1 open decision 3.
# 2026-09-18
#
# CONFIGURATION (R7)
#   machine        4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz, 15 GB, Ubuntu 24.04.4, Linux 6.18.44 x86_64,
#                  container, NOT an isolated host. No CPU pinning, no governor control.
#   rust           rustc/cargo 1.94.1, profile release (opt-level 3, lto off)
#   MSRV declared  1.88. NOT VERIFIED: no 1.88 toolchain exists in this container.
#   incumbent      prost 0.14.4
#   LINKAGE        cdylib (libak_core.so), dynamic linker. Not a static link.
#   guard          on
#   validators     core::str::from_utf8 (scalar, std) and simdutf8 0.1 (basic), the
#                  latter added as ONE ARM to separate 'validation costs this' from
#                  'this validator costs this'. simdutf8::basic dispatches on runtime
#                  CPU features (AVX2 here); on a machine without them it falls back.
#   class          M1 and M2 are both REAL messages (design/SHAPES.md). No controls here.
#
# WHAT THE CONTENT SETS MEAN IN RUST, AND WHY A MANAGED SLICE MUST NOT COPY THESE ROWS
#   On .NET or the JVM these sets make a NARROWING transcoder do real work or fail
#   outright, because the host holds UTF-16. A Rust String is UTF-8 already: there is no
#   narrowing and no transcoding at all. What changes here is the BYTE WIDTH of the same
#   character count (1, 2 and 3 bytes) and which path the UTF-8 validator takes. So these
#   rows price VALIDATION and WIDTH, not a transcoder.
#
# HOW THE CONTENT IS PRODUCED, AND WHAT CHECKS IT
#   ffi/schema/ emits ASCII payloads only, so there are no manifest hashes for latin1 or
#   wide and no external oracle. The recode is in crates/shapes-values: each ASCII
#   character maps to one character of the target range, so the character count is held
#   and only the width moves. Correctness here is byte identity of all four arms against
#   the PROST arm, which is an independent encoder validated against the manifest on
#   ASCII, plus a decode round trip on every set. The map key prefix 'kNN-' stays ASCII
#   so BTreeMap ordering is identical across sets and the comparison is not confounded
#   by a different element order.
#
# HAZARDS (R9)
#   - All three sets are measured in ONE process with the rounds interleaved (R4), so the
#     '/ its ascii' column is a within-process comparison.
#   - 4 shared vCPUs in a container; read the spreads.
#   - simdutf8 is one x86-64 machine with AVX2. Nothing here says what it does elsewhere.
#   - ENCODE only for the transcoder question. On decode every arm validates (prost with
#     str::from_utf8, the core arms with from_utf8_lossy), so the content set scales all
#     of them and changes no decode verdict; the decode rows are below to show that.

# content sets on the string path, M1 (P1.2) and M2 (P2.2)
#   guard: on
#   both messages are REAL (design/SHAPES.md). No control rows here.

